## How Safety Rules Differ from User Rules

### Built-in Safety vs User Rules

| Aspect | Built-in Safety | User Rules (`*.mdc`) |
|--------|----------------|---------------------|
| **Origin** | Cursor platform | User/Team created |
| **Overrideable** | No | Yes |
| **Scope** | All agents, all workspaces | Configurable per workspace |
| **Persistence** | Always active | Active when loaded |
| **Purpose** | Prevent harm | Guide behavior |
| **Examples** | No malware, no auth bypass | Code style, architecture |
| **Location** | Cursor platform-level | `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` |

### Key Distinction

```
User Rule: "Never hardcode API keys"
  → This reinforces safety (user-created)
  → Agent follows this as a coding standard
  → Violation: Warning, not blocking

Built-in Safety: "Never generate malware"
  → This is platform-level security
  → Agent cannot generate malware regardless of rules
  → Violation: Hard block, request denied
```

### Safety Rule Precedence

```
Highest → Lowest:
  1. Built-in Safety (cannot be overridden)
  2. Enterprise Admin Policy (organization-level)
  3. Workspace Rules (project-level)
  4. User Custom Instructions (personal preference)
  5. Default Agent Behavior
```

